Decide ground by tracing downward, not by the shape of the collision normal. (SIM-27)

⚠️ NOT APPLIED, AND NOT THE FIX. Written 2026-08-30 against a cause that turned out to be wrong.
It builds, it flies, and it changes nothing about the fault: three CitySample seeds split with it
in place exactly as they did without it. The real cause is the collision witness consuming a
read-and-reset flag before the physics engine sees it -- see the 2026-08-31 worklog and SIM-27.

It lives under experimental/ so the build scripts' *.patch glob does NOT pick it up. Kept rather
than deleted for two reasons: it does address a genuine minority mechanism (one seed of six
rejected the surface on a wall-shaped normal), and the runs carrying it tracked actor-vs-integrator
noticeably tighter (0.33-0.46 m) than stock did (0.68-0.84 m) -- unexplained, possibly worth
revisiting once the real fix lands.


THE DEFECT. FastPhysicsEngine has exactly one branch that stops a descent -- the ground lock,
which zeroes velocity and pins the body to the contact point. Reaching it requires the engine to
recognise the contact as ground, and it decides that ENTIRELY from the collision normal:

    const bool is_ground_normal = Utils::isApproximatelyEqual(std::abs(normal_body.z()), 1.0f,
                                                              kAxisTolerance);   // 0.25f

That asks "does this surface face up?", which is a proxy for "can I rest on this". On coarse
world geometry the proxy fails. MEASURED on Epic's CitySample (2026-08-29, three seeds, all
three affected): during a landing that never terminated, the contact reported |normal.z| between
0.00000 and 0.00086 -- a sideways normal, geometrically a wall -- 295 consecutive times over
141 s, while the vehicle sat on the surface the renderer showed beneath it. ground_collision
stayed false, the lock never engaged, and the integrator ran on downward at up to 2.45 m/s to
tens of metres below the world while the Unreal actor stayed put.

Every sensor PX4 receives is synthesised from that integrator, so the aircraft is told it is
still falling, never disarms, and the landing state times out. It presents as a control failure
on a flight that flew every waypoint.

WHY NOT WIDEN kAxisTolerance. Healthy touchdowns in the same three runs measure 0.99999-1.00000.
The two populations sit at opposite ends of the scale; no threshold separates them, and 0.25
versus 0.05 versus 0.9 changes nothing.

THE FIX. Trace one ray straight down from the vehicle and report whether anything solid is under
it. That question can only be asked where a world exists, so it is asked in PawnSimApi (Unreal
side) and carried to the engine as a flag on CollisionInfo, which is a plain struct with no
engine-specific types in it.

  1. CollisionInfo gains `bool is_ground` (defaults false -- anything that does not know says no)
  2. PawnSimApi::onCollision sets it from GetObstacle, straight down, 50 cm, ignoring the vehicle
  3. FastPhysicsEngine accepts EITHER signal: `collision_info.is_ground || <the normal test>`

ADDITIVE, NOT A REPLACEMENT. The upstream test remains as the fallback, so every world where it
already works -- Blocks, and any simulator that never sets the flag -- behaves exactly as before.
`is_landing` still gates the use of it, so "something underneath" only counts while the motion is
downward-dominant; no new threshold is introduced beyond the 50 cm probe.

WHY 50 cm: the landing gear puts the contact point ~32 cm below the vehicle origin (measured on
a default spawn -- body at NED +0.582, contact with the ground at +0.900). The probe reaches a
surface the vehicle is standing on and nothing further; in flight it finds nothing.

WHAT OWNS THE SURFACE IS DELIBERATELY NOT PART OF THE TEST. A rooftop, a bridge deck or a parked
car is as landable as a road. A rule that looked for the landscape would pass the seeds this was
measured against and break the first time someone landed on a building.

Paths are given under Unreal/Plugins/AirSim/Source/ because that is the tree the patch is applied
to (build_blocks.sh and convert_world.sh, via vp_apply_unreal -p4). Upstream's build.sh rsyncs
AirLib/ over Source/AirLib, so re-running it drops hunks 1 and 2 -- re-apply and rebuild, and
verify the strings are in the .so that LOADED, not the one on disk.

